 {id:"g5",title:"Keep a placement preparation log",text:"At the end of each week, write down what you studied, which problems you could not solve, and what you will revise next. This makes your preparation measurable instead of relying on motivation.",author:"Aarav Sharma · B.Tech CSE · Class of 2025 · Illustrative sample profile"},
 {id:"g6",title:"Do not prepare only for one company",text:"Build transferable foundations first: one programming language, DSA, core CS, communication and two explainable projects. Company-specific preparation should come after you understand the role and eligibility.",author:"Mehak Arora · BCA · Class of 2025 · Illustrative sample profile"},
 {id:"g7",title:"Practise speaking while solving",text:"Explain your approach aloud before writing code. Interviewers need to understand how you break down a problem, test edge cases and respond when your first approach fails.",author:"Ritvik Singh · B.Tech IT · Class of 2026 · Illustrative sample profile"},
 {id:"g8",title:"Make your resume evidence-based",text:"For every skill on your resume, keep one example ready: a project, assignment, internship, lab task or measurable result. Remove tools you cannot explain in a follow-up question.",author:"Sana Kapoor · MBA · Class of 2025 · Illustrative sample profile"},
 {id:"g9",title:"Use mock interviews to find blind spots",text:"After each mock interview, record the question, what you answered, what was missing and the revised answer. Repeat the same question a few days later to check whether you improved.",author:"Devansh Rao · B.Tech ECE · Class of 2025 · Illustrative sample profile"},
 {id:"g10",title:"For core branches, show practical proof",text:"Keep a concise portfolio of lab work, CAD/CAE models, simulations, prototypes, site visits or process-improvement work. Explain assumptions, constraints, safety and what you personally completed.",author:"Ishita Verma · B.Tech Mechanical · Class of 2025 · Illustrative sample profile"}
